// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD
//
// Registry 921, form 1 of 4: `@take(1, 2)` -- the `@` self-call form, one argument
// too many for `mut @take(n int)`. Before the fix this passed `nova check` and died
// in C with "too many arguments to function call".
//
// WHY THE CODE IS `E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD` AND NOT A NEW ONE. Arity is not a
// separate rule here, it is the same rule the checker already applies to argument
// TYPES: no overload of this method accepts what the call site offers. Giving the
// count its own code would put two names on one question, which is the disease
// row #894 was filed about. `E_FN_VALUE_CALL_ARITY` stays where it is, on the
// fn-VALUE path, where its own fixture explains why that case is different: a
// fn-value has no overload set at all, so there is nothing to resolve against.
//
// AND WHY THE CHECK IS NARROW ON PURPOSE. `p101_fn_value_call_arity_neg.nv` records
// the reason ordinary call arity is deferred to the C compiler: arity PARTICIPATES
// in overload resolution, so a blanket arity error would refuse legal programs. The
// fix does not lift that -- it fires only where NO overload matches the count, which
// is the case that has nothing left to resolve and therefore nothing to defer.
//
// One of FOUR fixtures, one per call form, plus a "too few" one and a positive
// control: the row's acceptance is class-level, and four forms sharing one door is
// the thing being tested.
module neg.p921_arity_method_at_form_neg

type Box priv { n int }
fn Box.new() -> Box => { n: 0 }
fn Box mut @take(n int) -> () { @n = n }

fn Box mut @drive() -> () {
    // nova:expect E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD -- `@take` declares one parameter and the call offers two; the `@` form must meet the same door as `x.m(...)` (#921)
    @take(1, 2)
}

fn main() Io -> () {
    mut b = Box.new()
    b.drive()
    println(b.n)
}
